menu "Knob shared UI (P1)"

config KNOB_UI_ENABLE
    bool "Link the shared clock UI (screens/settings) into the knob"
    default y
    help
        The P1 umbrella kill-switch. With y (the family posture) the knob
        boots the SHARED home screen (moon image + split-flap HH:MM +
        alarm row + seconds arc) through components/ui over the
        knob_moon_render.c seam. With n the knob keeps its K7
        own-screen moon view (disk + caption) and skips starting the
        shared UI -- the component still links (knob/CMakeLists adds
        ui unconditionally); the gate is the runtime check in main.c.

config KNOB_UI_LAST_FRAME
    bool "Show the persisted last frame before the fresh wake bake lands"
    default y
    help
        Deep sleep loses the PSRAM double buffer, so a dial/tap/TIMER
        wake otherwise starts from a black window while the re-bake
        waits behind the Wi-Fi + NTP window. With y the producer
        persists the last applied frame to /spiffs/moon_frame.bin on
        the 512 K storage partition (raw RGB565, 259,200 B payload +
        a 24-byte header: magic, baked epoch, UTC day key, event
        signature, crc32) -- and only when the (day, signature) pair
        changed, so the skip-write keeps the flash wear to one ~259 K
        rewrite per day or sky-event change, not one per wake. The
        next boot's first arm shows a frame at most 2 days old (older
        would be a visibly different phase) as ONE atomic whole-panel
        flush, the caption seeded from the saved instant (the shared
        home's gated row hides it; the legacy view rolls it with the
        minute like any day-tick frame); the fresh bake then lands
        over it as one atomic swap -- the old-frame-then-atomic
        posture the day tick keeps, now real across deep sleep. With no
        usable frame saved (the very first boot after a fresh flash
        wipes the file with the spiffs image) the boot instead
        publishes the quick phase disc first (the compute section's
        dummy, user 2026-11-24) and the fresh bake swaps over it.

config KNOB_UI_MOVIE_ENABLE
    bool "Play the flash-resident boot movie (the moon_movie partition) at boot"
    default y
    help
        The knob's half of the family boot re-source (2026-11-25, the
        clocks' UI_MOON_BOOT_REPLAY movie leg): the moon_movie data
        partition carries a precomputed synodic cycle of q90 gray JPEGs
        baked at build time by the portable core (python -m moon.movie
        --target knob), pixel-identical to a device bake. The knob has
        no SD socket -- flash is its ONLY movie source. With y the
        first arm stages frame 0 on the glass (born visible, the
        KNOB_UI_LAST_FRAME warm-arm shape; a saved frame takes
        precedence -- it is the latest real phase) and the producer
        plays the cycle forward over the Wi-Fi + NTP window + the
        opening bake window (ONE atomic whole-panel flush per frame,
        the s_buf[2] swap seam -- no new atomicity exception); when the
        clock holds a trustworthy instant the loop plays to the frame
        matching the current phase and the fresh bake reveals over it.
        No trustworthy instant by one full cycle: the loop exits and
        the opening bake reveals over the last movie frame (the day
        tick's late-fix re-bake lands the real phase). With n the boot
        keeps
        today's saved-frame / disc path. A missing / calcver-stale /
        zero-frame movie never blocks a flash or a boot (the fallback
        is the card-less posture the knob has always had).

config KNOB_UI_MOVIE_FPS
    int "Boot movie: target frame rate (fps)"
    depends on KNOB_UI_MOVIE_ENABLE
    default 8
    range 2 20
    help
        The loop's pacing floor (one frame per 1000/FPS ms, floored to
        one tick at CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ=100). The knob's wire floor is
        ~13 ms/frame, so 8 is well below the panel's own limit; the
        flash read of a q90 gray JPEG (360x360) plus the LUT pass costs
        a few ms per frame.

config KNOB_DIAG_FRAME_TRACE
    bool "Bench: log flush/swap/screen-switch boundaries"
    default n
    help
        Bench-only: ESP_LOGI at flush start/complete, swap-task apply
        begin/end, and each leg of the fade + atomic screen switch, so
        a frozen board's LAST log line names the section that stuck.

config KNOB_DIAG_PANIC_AT_FRAME
    int "Bench: abort() once the producer has published N frames (0 = off) — black-box self-test"
    default 0
    help
        Bench-only witness-chain proof (task #27): abort() after the
        boot static bake has published its frame (N = 1; the check
        rides the producer's opening presentation), so the boot after
        MUST print the gasp marker (stage/pub/applied) and the flash
        coredump must be readable with `idf.py -C knob coredump-read`.
        Proves the crash witness end to end without waiting for the
        unreproducible field crash. (The sweep-era name: the hook rode
        the boot sweep loop; it moved onto the static boot bake when
        the sweep retired 2026-11-14.) Production default 0 = dead.

config KNOB_TEST_CLASSIFY_STRESS
    int "Bench: hammer the erfa classify pass N times on boot (0 = off) — tick-ISR crash repro"
    default 0
    help
        Bench-only repro loop for the b924abf systimer-tick family (the
        knob_moon tick-ISR crash). After the boot presentation, the
        moon_task calls knob_moon_classify_signature() N times over a
        swept epoch range — the EXACT chain the coredump caught (
        moon_event_classify -> full_moments -> se_argmax -> se_dot ->
        moon_geometry -> eraEpv00) — with no bake side effects, keeping
        core 1 hot in erfa so the core-0 tick's cross-core critical
        snoop collides. Logs progress every 50 passes and a
        "classify-stress: PASS N" line on survival. Use it to A/B the
        knob_moon PSRAM-vs-internal stack and to raise the rate for the
        H2 (tick rate) / H3 (erfa buffer) hypotheses. Production default
        0 = dead.

endmenu
